"── 117_winui_examples.mst — WG8: doc examples that RUN ──────────────────
 `docs/winui-cookbook.md`'s blocks are extracted and evaluated against a live
 window by `just gate-cookbook`. This is the ten lines that make the result
 checkable instead of merely printed.

 WHY A FAILURE LIST rather than letting an error propagate. An unhandled error
 in example three would abort the run and say nothing about examples four
 through nine — so the gate would report one broken snippet when there might be
 five, and the next run would report the next one. Recording and continuing
 means one run tells you about all of them, which is the difference between a
 gate you fix in an afternoon and one you fix five times.

 WHY IT LIVES IN THE WORLD rather than in the driver. The driver is Tcl over a
 socket and can only see what a doit ANSWERS; an example that half-worked would
 answer fine. The handler is on this side of the seam because that is the only
 side that can see the exception."

Object subclass: WinShell [
    <classVars: ExampleFailures>

    WinShell class >> resetExampleFailures [
        ExampleFailures := OrderedCollection new.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> exampleFailures ^ <OrderedCollection> [
        ExampleFailures isNil ifTrue: [ self resetExampleFailures ].
        ^ExampleFailures
    ]

    WinShell class >> noteExampleFailure: aString <String> [
        self exampleFailures add: aString.
        "ALSO to the Transcript, because the gate reports a count and a count
         does not tell you which line to open. The Transcript is where someone
         watching the window finds out, and the two never disagree."
        self appendTranscript: 'cookbook: ' , aString.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> exampleFailureCount ^ <Integer> [
        ^self exampleFailures size ]

    WinShell class >> exampleFailureReport ^ <String> [
        "One line per failure, or the empty String. Answered to the gate, which
         prints it — a gate that failed with a bare `1 != 0` would be true and
         useless."
        | s |
        s := WriteStream on: String new.
        self exampleFailures do: [ :each | s nextPutAll: each ] separatedBy: [ s nextPutAll: ' | ' ].
        ^s contents
    ]
]
